comments.body:
Thanks for tracking this down, @pcbeingused333! Stamping inline images at parse time is the right place for this fix.

I merged the newest `main` and pushed two follow-up commits (1104c4a4, defabf87):

- **The rule now reaches wrapped images.** An image is inline when its nearest paragraph, heading or table cell holds anything besides images, at any depth. This reuses `hasNonImageContent`. A linked badge in a sentence (`See [![badge](b.svg)](url) for status`), `text **![](x)**` and `||photo ![](x) here||` now render inline, as they do on iOS and Android. An image alone in its paragraph, or wrapped only in a link, stays a block.
- **Spoilers:** #1225 drew a full-width cover for a spoiler holding text and an image, because that image rendered as a block, and documented this as a web limitation. Those images are now inline, so I updated `spoiler.test.tsx` deliberately. I also removed the full-width cover branch, the unused `hasBlockImage` helper, and the limitation note in the web docs. Image-only spoilers keep their own cover.
- **Tests:** I replaced the hand-built AST test with `src/web/__tests__/inlineImages.test.tsx`. It renders real markdown through the parser and checks each image's layout:
  - the issue's example;
  - a linked badge in a sentence;
  - bold text;
  - a heading;
  - a table cell;
  - an image-only spoiler next to text;
  - the two block cases.

  Most of these fail on `main`.
- I updated the title to describe the rule.

Two existing differences from native are unchanged: an image that starts a line and is followed by text, and adjacent images with no text between them.

Verified: the full jest suite (2100 tests), `typecheck` and `eslint` pass. Thanks again for the contribution!
